  "media-stream": {
    section: "terms",
    name: "MediaStream and MediaStreamTrack",
    short: "A MediaStream is a bundle of live tracks; a MediaStreamTrack is one of them, a single source of video or sound.",
    what: "Capture APIs hand pages <code>MediaStream</code>s: a camera stream with one video and one audio track, a screen stream, a canvas stream. Each <code>MediaStreamTrack</code> has a <code>kind</code> (audio or video), a <code>label</code> (often the device's name) and a <code>readyState</code>, <code>live</code> or <code>ended</code>. A track can be <i>muted</i> by its source (no data for now) or disabled by the page.",
    how: "A track describes itself three ways: <code>getConstraints()</code> (what the page asked for), <code>getSettings()</code> (what it's doing) and <code>getCapabilities()</code> (what it could do). The bench's track table shows them as Asked, Got and Can do, and tags a row <b>not as asked</b> where Got differs. It logs each track's <code>ended</code>, <code>mute</code> and <code>unmute</code>; when every track has ended, the take finishes. While a track is muted or disabled, a recorder records black frames or silence.",
    best: "Watch the track events in the log: an <code>ended</code> you didn't expect means the source went away, such as a camera being unplugged or a share being stopped.",
    support: "All four browsers have them. Firefox has had <code>getCapabilities()</code> only since version 132, so before that its Can do column stays empty.",
    related: ["constraints", "source-camera", "api-MediaRecorder", "how-a-take-works"],
  },
  constraints: {
    section: "terms",
    name: "Constraints (ideal, exact, min, max)",
    short: "How a page says what it wants from a camera or microphone: a plain value or <code>ideal</code> is a wish; <code>exact</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> are requirements.",
    what: "Each constraint names a setting (width, frameRate, echoCancellation…) and gives a value. A bare value counts as <code>ideal</code>: the browser gets as close as it can, and never fails because of it. <code>exact</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> must be met, or the request fails with an OverconstrainedError.",
    how: "The browser scores every setting the device could use by how far it is from the ideals (the spec's <i>fitness distance</i>), drops any that break a requirement, and picks the closest. Where nothing was asked it uses the device's or its own defaults; the spec notes these tend to be 640 by 480 at 30 frames per second, with echo cancellation on. A constraint the browser doesn't know is silently ignored, so <code>getSupportedConstraints()</code> is the way to tell. <code>getDisplayMedia()</code> doesn't allow <code>exact</code> or <code>min</code> at all.",
    best: "Ask with plain values first and read what you got; use <b>strict</b> only when you need a guarantee.",
    related: ["strict", "OverconstrainedError", "group-start", "media-stream", "width", "echoCancellation"],
  },
